From 12fbfc0533f33b2c76e70b0ef61133f73acec2cc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Theorem Labs
Date: Wed, 19 Aug 2026 17:01:47 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [compiler-rt] sealed init: a start-up that reads nothing
 outside the binary
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

🤖: A caller who reasons about one of these binaries formally has to quantify
over what the process is handed -- every valid environment, every valid initial
filesystem, every valid initial stack pointer -- and ASan's start-up reads all
three.  Measured on a static riscv64 binary that does one write(2) and _exit(0),
under qemu-riscv64-static -strace, a healthy run makes seventeen kinds of
syscall.  Ten of them are read, write, openat, close, mmap, munmap, brk,
readlinkat, set_tid_address and exit_group.  The other seven are

  madvise (6)   newfstatat (5)   sigaltstack (4)   rt_sigaction (3)
  prlimit64 (3) rt_sigprocmask (1) getpid (1)

and on top of those it opens /proc/self/cmdline once, /proc/self/environ once
and /proc/self/maps four times, all through syscalls in the first list.

The /proc reads are the ones that matter most, and a syscall census does not
show why.  ASan does not call getenv: on Linux, GetEnv() reads
/proc/self/environ directly.  So ASAN_OPTIONS is read on every run, and
ASAN_OPTIONS=verbosity=1 is a legal environment under which an instrumented
binary writes to stderr where the uninstrumented one is silent -- an
observable difference with nothing to do with memory safety.  /proc/self/maps
is a file, and an initial filesystem in which it holds something else is a legal
filesystem.

This adds a mode in which none of that happens.  It is off unless the link asks
for it, so nothing about an ordinary build changes:

  clang --config=/opt/asan-static/asan-musl-sealed.cfg prog.c -o prog

which is the ordinary static ASan link plus
-Wl,--defsym=__sanitizer_sealed_init=1.  sanitizer_sealed.h holds the switch,
the two riscv64 constants and the whole of the reasoning; what follows is what
each site does with it.

The environment is sealed at GetEnv(), not at its callers.  ASAN_OPTIONS,
LSAN_OPTIONS, UBSAN_OPTIONS and ASAN_SYMBOLIZER_PATH all arrive there, so one
branch covers every name the runtime reads today and every one added later,
which is the only version of this that stays true.  Options then come from the
compiled-in defaults and from __asan_default_options(), both of which are parts
of the binary.  GetArgsAndEnv(), which is the /proc/self/cmdline and second
/proc/self/environ read (musl has no __libc_stack_end, so the fallback arm is
the only arm on this libc), hands back empty vectors.  CacheBinaryName() names
the binary <sealed> instead of resolving /proc/self/exe and reading
/proc/self/cmdline.

Three /proc/self/maps reads go, one per caller.  AsanCheckIncompatibleRT()
walks the maps looking for a libclang_rt.asan.so mapped alongside the static
runtime; a binary this mode is for is statically linked with no PT_INTERP, so
nothing mapped one before the program started and there is no loader in it to
map one later.  MemoryRangeIsAvailable() asks whether the shadow collides with
an existing mapping; sealed mode answers yes without looking, which is what
ASan already assumes on every platform whose shadow offset is dynamic.
GetThreadStackTopAndBottom() finds the main thread's stack by walking the maps
for the segment containing a local, and reads getrlimit(RLIMIT_STACK) for how
far down it may grow -- a /proc read and one of the three prlimit64 calls.
Sealed mode uses a fixed 8MB window, which is Linux's default RLIMIT_STACK,
ending at the frame __asan_init recorded on its way in, rounded up to a page.

Both halves of that are load-bearing.  Not the frame that computes it: what the
bound has to contain is a local of SetThreadStackAndTls, one of the callers of
the function computing it and so at a higher address, which AsanThread checks
with CHECK(AddrIsInStack(&local)).  Rounding the computing frame up to a page
usually covers the callers in between and does not when that frame lands within
a few hundred bytes below a page boundary -- so the seal would abort at start-up for some
sizes of the environment and not others, which is exactly the kind of dependence
this mode exists to remove.  And no further than a page: a bound past the top of
the mapped stack is worse than a small one, because on riscv64 HighMem ends
where the stack does, so shadow writes for an address above it would land
outside the shadow and in somebody else's memory.  What stays outside the window
is main's own frame, which sits beside __libc_start_main's rather than below it;
that costs the "located in stack of thread T0" line of a report and nothing
else, since a frame above this one is a frame that is still live and
__asan_handle_no_return is about the ones below.

Symbolizer::LateInitialize() is not called.  All it does early is what the
first report would do anyway -- Symbolizer::GetOrInit() is on the report path
too -- and doing it early costs a stat of every directory in PATH looking for
llvm-symbolizer, which is a newfstatat and a look at the filesystem on a run
that may never report anything.  (With the environment sealed there is no PATH,
so what it actually stats is the relative name llvm-symbolizer, in whatever the
working directory is: a filesystem read whose result an initial filesystem
chooses.)  Deferred, it lands where the rest of symbolization already is.

The rest are one line each.  internal_madvise() returns 0 without the syscall:
every madvise this runtime issues is advice -- MADV_DONTNEED to give freed pages
back, MADV_DONTDUMP and MADV_[NO]HUGEPAGE on the shadow -- so sealing the one
place they are issued costs resident memory and nothing else.
PlatformUnpoisonStacks(), which runs on the way out of every program that calls
a noreturn function and is where the fourth sigaltstack comes from, returns
false: no alternate stack was installed, so there is nothing to ask.
CreateMainThread() gives the main thread the os_id 0 rather than a getpid().

The signal handlers, the alternate signal stack and the core dumper are flags
already, and sealed mode pins them -- handle_segv, handle_sigbus, handle_abort,
handle_sigill, handle_sigtrap, handle_sigfpe, use_sigaltstack, disable_coredump
-- after every other source of flag values, so the guarantee belongs to the link
rather than to what the program remembered to ask for.  Two more are pinned for
reasons of their own.  detect_leaks, because the leak check runs at exit, which
is on every run and not only a failing one, and cannot be sealed at all:
StopTheWorld clones a tracer and ptraces the process, and the scan walks
/proc/self/maps.  A mode whose start-up is sealed and whose exit is not would be
no mode at all.  And the three background-thread flags, off by default, because
turning any of them on starts a thread before main.

One more is pinned and it is the only one here that is not about start-up:
allocator_release_to_os_interval_ms, to -1.  The primary allocator's
MaybeReleaseToOS() reads MonotonicNanoTime(), a clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC),
once a size class has a page's worth of freed chunks -- so it appears on no
syscall census taken from a program that allocates little, which is every census
in this file.  Sealing start-up cannot reach it, because it is not start-up.
Measured on riscv64/musl under qemu-riscv64-static -strace, 800 malloc/free pairs
across varied size classes: 23 clock_gettime before, 0 after.

One more pin is not a syscall a healthy run makes at all: abort_on_error, to 1.
It is how a run that FOUND a bug dies.  A detection's report ends in Die(),
which otherwise leaves by exit_group(exitcode) -- and an exit status is a
channel the program itself also has, so a caller comparing this binary's
syscalls against its plain twin's cannot tell that exit from one the program
chose.  Abort() dies by SIGABRT, whose rt_sigprocmask and tkill nothing on a
healthy run emits, so what killed the process is not a status the program
could have returned, and a detection is unambiguous to every observer.  What
is not sealed stays not sealed: the report itself still prints, the abort's
own two syscalls are accepted, because a run that found a bug has already
left the behaviour sealing is about -- what the pin changes is only the last
event, from an exit the program could have made to a signal it could not.
An unsealed binary is untouched; its Die() keeps the upstream default.
Pinned in SealFlags(), after __asan_default_options() has been parsed, a
program's hook cannot reset it; that ordering is load-bearing for a consumer
whose hook pins allocator_may_return_null=1 and must not unpin this.

A 32-bit target has the same shape of problem in a second place, and it is
answered the same way.  GetKernelAreaSize(), which is how GetMaxUserVirtualAddress()
finds the top of memory there, probes the running kernel: /proc/self/maps for a
writable segment in the top gigabyte, then personality() and uname() for whether
a 64-bit kernel is underneath.  Sealed mode answers with the gigabyte, which is
the 3G/1G split every 32-bit Linux this targets uses and the one ASan's own
32-bit mapping is written for, with HighMem ending at 0xbfffffff.  Measured on
armv7: that is the last /proc read in a sealed run, and the personality and
uname calls go with it.

That leaves riscv64's shadow, which is not a flag and is the reason this is a
patch rather than a configuration.  asan_mapping.h selects
ASAN_SHADOW_OFFSET_DYNAMIC there, the offset comes from an mmap probe sized by
GetMaxVirtualAddress(), and on riscv64 that function answers
(1 << (msb(current frame) + 1)) - 1 -- because RISC-V has Sv39, Sv48 and Sv57
and nothing tells a process which one it is under.  So the size of the shadow
reservation is a function of the initial stack pointer, which is the variable a
caller of this kind quantifies over: every proof would have to reason about a
mapping the caller does not choose.  Sealed mode pins GetMaxVirtualAddress() to
0x7fffffffffff and FindDynamicShadowStart() to 0x7fff8000, which is the Sv48
layout: Linux/riscv64 puts STACK_TOP at DEFAULT_MAP_WINDOW = 1 <<
(MMAP_VA_BITS - 1) and MMAP_VA_BITS saturates at 48, so the top user address is
0x7fffffffffff on any kernel with VA_BITS >= 48, and the offset is the one
asan_mapping.h already uses for every other 47-bit user address space,
x86_64's included.

Sv39 is the other plausible pin and it is the wrong one, which is worth saying
because it is the tempting one: it is what much riscv64 hardware runs and it
makes the reservation 4GB rather than 14TB.  But a caller reasoning about these
binaries quantifies the initial stack pointer over the window a kernel can put
it in, and on an Sv48 kernel every such address is above an Sv39 ceiling -- so
an Sv39-sealed binary would take the diagnostic path on every admissible run.
A seal that refuses every run it was built for is worse than no seal, and
nothing in a census would have shown it: the census is of a run that starts.

The emulated case is how the frame-derived answer is most easily seen, and it is
also why the pin has to be this one: under qemu-user the guest stack sits at a
host address, so on a 47-bit host GetMaxVirtualAddress() answers 0x7fffffffffff
and the reservation is 16TB.  The pin makes that the constant rather than the
measurement.

Pinning has a cost, on both architectures that do it, and __asan_init now states
it rather than letting it fail later: a process whose stack is above the top of
memory the binary carries cannot be sanitized by that binary, because HighMem
ends below the stack and a shadow write for a stack address lands past the end
of the shadow.  On riscv64 that is a kernel with VA_BITS >= 48, which places the
initial stack near 1<<47, and it is qemu-user without -R 0x4000000000; on a
32-bit target it is a kernel whose user/kernel split is not 3G/1G.  The check is
against the frame __asan_init runs on, and it names which case it is, rather
than dying inside a CHECK in asan_poisoning.cpp that does not mention the
address space or, past that, quietly writing over memory that is not shadow.

What is deliberately not sealed is the path a report takes.  Symbolization, the
report and everything after it may do whatever they do: a run that reports has
already left the behaviour the caller reasons about, and giving that path a
quiet mode would remove the signal rather than the syscall.  Sealing is about
start-up and teardown, which happen on every input.

SealFlags() pins four more flags, each a way for a healthy run's behaviour to
depend on something other than the program and its input.  malloc_context_size
is 1: at its default of 30, every malloc and free walks up to 30 frames of the
caller's frame-pointer chain and stores the trace in the stack depot, so the
depot grows with the number of distinct call paths and depends on what the
stack holds.  At 1 the allocator records only the pc of the call, with no
walk, and the depot holds one trace per allocation site.  compress_stack_depot
is 0, so the depot never starts its compression thread (a clone).
print_module_map is 0 and decorate_proc_maps is false: the first reads
/proc/self/maps and the second names mappings with prctl.  A report loses its
allocation and free stacks beyond the first frame; a sealed binary that reports
has already left the behaviour this mode is for.  Like the other pins, these
are applied after __asan_default_options() is parsed, so a hook cannot unpin
them.

cf is declared [[clang::uninitialized]]: CopyFrom() overwrites all of it, so
the pattern fill -ftrivial-auto-var-init would emit first is dead.  Leaving it
out pays for the four stores, so InitializeFlags() keeps its size and no other
code in a linked binary moves.
